# {{label}}

A Greentic Designer Extension that surfaces an already-published WASM
component as a node in the flow-editor palette.

This is a `DesignExtension` with two entries under `runtime.components`:

| component | what it is |
|---|---|
| `{{runtime_ref_key}}` | this crate's design-time `extension.wasm`, shipped inside the `.gtxpack` |
| `{{runtime_ref_key}}-node` | the component that **executes** the node, referenced by `oci_ref` |

`contributions.nodeTypes[0].runtime_ref` points at the second one.

## The node component must be reachable by `oci_ref`

Not by a local `.gtpack`, and this is not a style preference — two layers
disagree with the local shape:

- the designer's flow compiler reads
  `runtime.components.<runtime_ref>.oci_ref` and **skips a `gtpack`-only
  component**, falling through to the catalog pin;
- the install path only relocates a nested `.gtpack` into the runner's pack
  directory for a `ProviderExtension`.

So a node backed by an in-pack `.gtpack` builds, installs, and never runs.

## Quickstart

1. Publish your component (world
   `greentic:component/component-v0-v6-v0@0.6.0`) to an OCI registry.
2. Put its reference in `describe.json` under
   `runtime.components.{{runtime_ref_key}}-node.oci_ref`, **pinned by
   digest** — a built pack embeds the ref permanently, and these registries
   publish tags out of chronological order, so the highest semver is
   frequently the oldest artifact. Scaffold it directly with
   `gtdx new --kind wasm-component --component-ref oci://…@sha256:…`.
3. Set the component's `sha256`, and fill in `category`, `icon`,
   `config_schema` and any `permissions` the node needs.
4. Check `contributions.nodeTypes[0].operation` names the operation your
   component exposes. The runner **requires** it whenever the component has
   more than one, and refuses the node at execution time — the palette, the
   flow builder and the pack build all report success first.
5. `gtdx dev` to build, install locally, and watch.
6. Open the designer — the node should be in the palette.
7. `gtdx publish` when ready.

## What this crate's own wasm is for

Design-time only: validation, prompt fragments, knowledge entries, and any
design-time tools you declare in `contributions.tools`. It never executes the
node. A fresh scaffold stubs all of it — you can leave it as-is if the node
needs no authoring affordances.
